th='Evelyn Glennie – a deaf percussionist who “hears” music through her body; Ustad Bismillah Khan – shehnai maestro who loved India; the wind as a friend of the strong (poem)',
sum='Part I: Evelyn Glennie, a Scottish percussionist, became profoundly deaf by the age of eleven (her deafness was first noticed at eight and confirmed at eleven). Though advised to go to a school for the deaf, she was determined to lead a normal life. Percussionist Ron Forbes saw her potential; he tuned two large drums to different notes and asked her to feel the sound – she felt the higher drum from the waist up and the lower one from the waist down. At sixteen she decided to make music her life; at seventeen she joined the Royal Academy of Music, London (scoring one of the highest marks) and won most of the top awards. She plays barefoot to feel vibrations through her body; she won the Royal Philharmonic Society’s Soloist of the Year award (1991), gives free concerts in prisons and hospitals and inspires deaf children. Part II: Emperor Aurangzeb banned the pungi for its shrill sound; a barber of a family of professional musicians made a longer, broader pipe with seven holes – the shehnai (“played in the Shah’s chamber by a nai”), auspicious at weddings and temples. Bismillah Khan (born 1916, Dumraon, Bihar) learnt from his uncle Ali Bux, practised at the Balaji and Mangala Maiya temples and on the banks of the Ganga in Benaras. He played on All India Radio (Lucknow, 1938), and on 15 August 1947 he greeted Independence from the Red Fort. He played abroad (first trip to Afghanistan), in films, received the Padma awards and the Bharat Ratna (2001). He refused to settle in the USA because he could not leave Benaras, the Ganga and Dumraon. Poem “Wind” (Subramania Bharati, translated by A.K. Ramanujan): the wind breaks shutters, scatters papers, tears books and crushes weak things; we should build strong homes, bodies and hearts – then the wind will be our friend; it blows out weak fires but makes strong fires roar.',
te='ఎవెలిన్ గ్లెనీ 11 ఏళ్లకే దాదాపు పూర్తిగా వినికిడి కోల్పోయినా, శరీరంతో ధ్వని ప్రకంపనలను అనుభవిస్తూ ప్రపంచ ప్రసిద్ధ పెర్కషన్ (తాళ వాద్య) కళాకారిణి అయింది. ఉస్తాద్ బిస్మిల్లా ఖాన్ షెహనాయిని శాస్త్రీయ వాద్యంగా ప్రపంచానికి పరిచయం చేశారు; భారతరత్న పొందారు; బనారస్, గంగను విడిచి అమెరికా వెళ్లడానికి నిరాకరించారు. “విండ్” కవితలో గాలి బలహీనమైనవాటిని నాశనం చేస్తుంది – బలమైన ఇళ్లు, శరీరం, మనసు నిర్మించుకుంటే గాలి మనకు స్నేహితుడవుతుంది.',
