 (33, 'Dictionary work; Speaking', [
  'IV. Only before noun: outdoor, paternal, countless, priceless | Not before noun: afraid | Both before and after “be”: indispensable, impressed.',
  'Sentences: The mobile phone has become indispensable. | Paternal care is important for children. | There are countless stars in the sky. | Our freedom is priceless. | I am afraid of dogs. | We enjoy outdoor games. | The teacher was impressed by her speech.',
  'Speaking I (notes): Parentage – daughter of Smt. Mogubai Kurdikar | School – Jaipur-Atrauli gharana (her own variant) | Achievements – finest female vocalist of her generation, revived the study of khayal | Inspiration – teachings of ancient Vedic sages | Awards – Sangeet Natak Akademi (1985), Padma Bhushan (1987), Sangeet Samradhini (1997).']),
 (34, 'Speaking II; Writing', [
  'II. Introduction: “Good morning everyone. I feel privileged to introduce Padma Bhushan Smt. Kishori Amonkar, the finest female vocalist of her generation. Born in 1931, she is the daughter of the great singer Mogubai Kurdikar. Trained in the Jaipur-Atrauli gharana, she developed her own unique style… She has received the Sangeet Natak Akademi Award, the Padma Bhushan and the Sangeet Samradhini Award. Please welcome her with a big round of applause.”',
  'Writing (two paragraphs): Evelyn Glennie worked very hard – she overcame deafness, learnt to feel music through her body, practised long hours (“I’ve just got to work…”) and wanted to “go” to the top as a solo percussionist and to inspire handicapped people. She reached there – won awards and gave pleasure to millions.',
  'Bismillah Khan practised for hours on the banks of the Ganga and in temples from childhood. He wanted to take the shehnai, a folk instrument, to the classical stage and to the world. He reached his goal – played at the Red Fort in 1947, performed abroad and received the Bharat Ratna – while staying rooted in Benaras.']),
 (36, 'Wind – Thinking about the Poem', [
  'I. 1. The wind breaks the shutters of the windows, scatters the papers, throws down the books from the shelf, tears their pages and brings rain.',
  '2. Yes – farmers winnow paddy in the field / mothers winnow rice at home. Telugu word: “తూర్పారబట్టడం” (చేట – winnowing basket is used).',
  '3. The wind god winnows frail, crumbling houses, doors, rafters, wood, bodies, lives and hearts – everything weak.',
  '4. Build strong homes, join the doors firmly, practise to make the body strong and make the heart steadfast.',
  '5. The wind blows out weak fires but makes strong fires roar and flourish – difficulties destroy the weak but make the strong even stronger. So we should become strong; then the wind’s friendship is good and we praise it.',
  '6. The poet speaks to the wind with humour (“You’re very clever at poking fun at weaklings”), not in anger. Response: When cyclones destroy lives (e.g., on the Andhra coast), I feel sad; like the poet, I feel we must build strong houses and be mentally strong to face disasters.',
  'II. Telugu poems on nature/wind – e.g., poems from “తెలుగు పరిమళం”; students may recite one.']),
